    "proved": "The Hausdorff comparison defined through isometric embeddings has explicit parameter bounds for the bounded weighted ordinary and ideal model loci, even across different active sets. The comparison equals the difference of weighted areas for componentwise ordered coefficients, and equals the sum of weighted areas at the singleton limit. Coefficient-decreasing maps are surjective contractions commuting with ideal projection, with exact discarded-coordinate fiber diameters. Every locus with a positive curve coefficient is noncompact.",
    "mechanism": "A direct bridge metric on a disjoint union proves the correspondence-distortion upper bound without a compactness assumption. Diameter comparison gives the lower bound. Common model tuples give a correspondence with distortion controlled by the weighted curve areas; the two bounds agree for ordered coefficients. Coordinate uniqueness and polar-total-variation distances compute the transition maps and their fibers. Infinitely many separated atomic models prove failure of total boundedness.",
    "retained_boundary": "Only the specified separated curve product locus is covered. No unrestricted higher-dimensional metric or capacity assertion upstairs is claimed. The top-volume comparison expression vanishes, including at the nonbig limit; no big-background theorem is applied there."
  },
